<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pinara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pinara was a city of ancient Lycia on the western side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanthos">Xanthos</a> valley in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and one of the six largest members of the Lycian League. It lies below a sheer cliff honeycombed with hundreds of rock-cut tombs, and its ruins include a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, an odeon and Lycian house-tombs with carved reliefs. The city declined in the Byzantine period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pinara.jpg" alt="Pinara" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gisela s · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theater_Pinara_T%C3%BCrkei.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pinara lies at the foot of Mount Cragus, now Mount Babadağ, not far from the River Xanthos, now the Eşen River, above the present-day village of Minare in the Fethiye district of Muğla Province. Its Lycian name, Pilleñni, is thought to come from a word for round, and the town does sit on a great round mass of rock. According to the Lycian history of Menecrates, quoted by Stephanus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, it was a colony of Xanthos first called Artymnesos, while Panyassis named an eponymous founder, Pinarus. A cult of Pandarus, the Lycian hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, was kept there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ancient writers seldom mention the city, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> counted it among the largest in Lycia, and it was a port until its harbour silted up into the reed-filled wetlands of today. It held three votes in the Lycian League and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pixodarus">Pixodarus</a> of Caria. Pinara surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 334 BCE, later passed to the kingdom of Pergamum, and became Roman when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> left Pergamum to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 133 BCE. It prospered under Roman rule but was badly damaged by earthquakes in 141 and 240 CE; after the first, Opramoas paid for repairs to its public buildings.</p><p>The city was Christianized early, and five of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> are known, from Eustathius at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> in 359 to Athanasius at the Photian Council of 879. It was the birthplace of Nicolas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a>. Under repeated pressure from invaders, it lost its inhabitants in the 9th century, and it survives as a titular see of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins were identified by Sir Charles Fellows, who described the round cliff speckled all over with tombs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in a very perfect state, with all its seats remaining. The site keeps the remains of several temples, a royal tomb, an upper and a lower acropolis, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> and a church, with inscriptions in Lycian and Greek.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pinara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Xanthos, Patara, <b>Pinara</b>, Olympos, Myra and Tlos were among its six largest members.</blockquote><a href="/state/lycian-league">Lycian League</a></li></ul></section>
